            <div xml:id="d273">

                <pb facs="0608" n="569" xml:id="pg569"/>

                <head>273. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Morocco<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d273-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">28881.</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1988-01-30T02:03:00+00:00">January 30, 1988, 0203Z</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>FY 88 Security Assistance Allocations for
                        Morocco: Special Talking Points.</item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Confidential—Entire text.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>You will have received by septel final FY
                        88 security assistance figures for all countries and extensive talking
                        points for briefing host governments.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d273-f2"/> The
                        following points may also be used in briefing the GOM on the unique features of the Moroccan FY 88 program: <list>
                            <label>—</label>
                            <item>Morocco did exceptionally, almost uniquely, well in a period of
                                major retrenchment;</item>
                            <label>—</label>
                            <item>Morocco’s FY 88 ESF, MAP, and FMS total 72
                                million dollars compared with 54 million dollars in FY 87;</item>
                            <label>—</label>
                            <item>In comparison, the administration’s overall request was cut 14
                                percent and the net after earmarks was a 93 percent cut;</item>
                            <label>—</label>
                            <item>In 30 countries, security assistance will be eliminated; nearly
                                all countries, including very important ones, will take massive
                                cuts. For example, our ESF request
                                for Turkey of 120 million was cut to 32. The actual Turkish program
                                in 87 was 100 million. Jordan will receive 26.5 million of a
                                requested 40 million, a substantial cut from the 39.9 million FY 87 actual level;</item>
                            <label>—</label>
                            <item>In sum, these results show, if it needed to be shown, the high
                                regard with which Morocco is held in the U.S.</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Shultz</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d273-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d273-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File, Electronic Telegrams,
                    D880081–0376. Confidential; Immediate. Drafted by Ralph Winstanley (NEA/AFN); cleared by William McGlynn (NEA/ECON), Ralph Boyce (D), Benjamin Pascoe (S/S), and
                    Carolyn Huggins (S/S–O); approved by Ussery.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d273-f2" n="2" xml:id="d273-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 29684 to Vienna, February 4, which repeated telegram 29684 to all
                    American Republic diplomatic posts, February 3, the Department outlined the
                    approved FY 88 security assistance allocations.
                    (Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File, Electronic Telegrams,
                    D880624–0215)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
